-- EXPECT-ERROR: `root_cap` cannot be referenced
-- R2 — THE ROOT CAPABILITY IS GRANTED, NOT TAKEN (2026-08-05).
--
-- `root_cap` was an ordinary global of type `Cap(IO)`, in scope in every
-- module. Measured before this change: a module declaring NO `needs` at all
-- could write `let x = root_cap` and hold the root capability, with no
-- diagnostic naming it. Narrowing from there to any descendant was then free
-- AND LEGITIMATE, because `cap_narrow` demands exactly the parent the module
-- now held.
--
-- That is no-authority-from-nothing violated at the source rather than at the
-- construction site, and it is why the claim earned by R3 had to be stated as
-- "a capability cannot be fabricated FROM DATA" rather than the shorter "a
-- capability can only be received" — nothing was received here.
--
-- The runtime already grants the root to `main` (see accept/t147); it is the
-- ambient global that had to go. Note this module declares `needs IO`, so it
-- is not the `needs` check firing: a fully-declared module still may not help
-- itself to the root.
mod TakesTheRoot do
  needs IO

  fn main() do
    let stolen = root_cap
    println("took the root capability without being given it")
  end
end
